# The one way to build BlitzLLVM, on every platform, for people and for CI alike: the CI workflows call this script too, so a build on
# your computer is configured, compiled and installed with the same tools and the same options as the nightly builds.
#
#   cmake -P tools/build.cmake -- [options]      (or tools/build.sh [options] on Linux and macOS, tools\build.cmd [options] on Windows)
#
# What it does, in order:
# 1. Tools: downloads the pinned Ninja (all platforms) and llvm-mingw (Windows) into .tools/ of the source tree, checked against their
#    SHA-256, unless they are there already. It is put first on PATH and passed to CMake, so no other Ninja is used (such as
#    the one of MSYS2's /usr/bin).
# 2. Dependencies: checks what the build needs from the system (MSYS2 and its packages for FFmpeg and NASM on Windows, the development
#    packages on Linux, NASM on macOS). With --install-deps it installs what is missing (pacman, apt-get with sudo, brew); without it, it
#    says what to install and stops.
# 3. Configure: the build directory with Ninja and Release, and on Windows llvm-mingw's clang and lld for the MinGW target.
# 4. Build.
# 5. Optional: --test runs ctest, --install <dir> installs into <dir> (the zip layout), with LICENSE.adoc and README.adoc.
#
# Options:
#   --build-dir <dir>     Build directory (default: build). Relative paths are relative to the source tree.
#   --config <name>       Release (default), Debug, RelWithDebInfo or MinSizeRel.
#   --arch <arch>         Windows: x64 or arm64 (default: this computer's). macOS: a list such as "arm64;x86_64" or arm64,x86_64 (default, universal).
#   --install-deps        Install missing system packages instead of stopping.
#   --relaxed             Build without what is optional (NASM, the HTML help, the Direct3D 11 shaders) when it is missing, instead of
#                         failing as the nightly builds do.
#   --system-libraries    Linux: let the dependencies come from the distribution where pkg-config finds them (BLITZ_SYSTEM_*=AUTO).
#                         The default builds all of them from the submodules, like the Flatpaks.
#   --gpu-tests           Also register the tests that need a window and a GPU (-DBLITZ_TEST_GPU=ON).
#   --version <A.B.C.D>   The version to build (default: from the commit date, see cmake/project_version.cmake).
#   --fresh               Configure from scratch (cmake --fresh), forgetting the cached settings of the build directory.
#   --configure-only      Stop after configuring.
#   --test                Run the tests after building.
#   --install <dir>       Install into <dir> after building.
#   --tools-only          Only get the tools (step 1) and print where they are, then stop.
#   --tools-dir <dir>     Where the downloaded tools go (default: .tools in the source tree).
#   --msys2 <dir>         Windows: where MSYS2 is (default: the environment variable BLITZLLVM_MSYS2, then C:/msys64).
#   -D<var>=<value>       Passed on to the configure step as it is.
#
# On GitHub Actions (GITHUB_ACTIONS=true) the folder of llvm-mingw is also added to GITHUB_PATH, so that the later steps of the job use the
# same clang. That is the only thing that the script does differently there.

